    <p>If you'd rather answer questions than edit placeholders, you can <a href="/">build one for free with the privacy policy generator</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="customize">How to Customize the Template</h2>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>List every tool that touches client data.</strong> Replace the bracketed service names and delete lines you don't use. A <a href="/tools/privacy-policy-checker/">privacy policy checker</a> can flag integrations you missed.</li>
      <li><strong>Check what really happens to EXIF data.</strong> In Lightroom Classic, the export dialog's Metadata section has a Remove Location Info option. Check your export presets and gallery settings, then make the template say what actually happens.</li>
      <li><strong>Set retention periods you will actually follow.</strong> Check how long your tax authority requires you to keep invoices, then pick a raw-file period that fits your workflow.</li>
      <li><strong>Match your model release.</strong> Section 3's portfolio wording should match what your release grants.</li>
      <li><strong>Publish it</strong> on its own page and link it from the places listed below.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="photography-clauses">Clauses Generic Templates Miss</h2>

    <p><strong>Model releases are data records too.</strong> A release covers using someone's image, not how you store their name, signature, and address. The privacy policy covers that.</p>

    <p><strong>Geotags in delivered files.</strong> GPS coordinates from a newborn or family session can show a client's home address. Say whether you strip location data, and where.</p>

    <p><strong>Wedding and event guests.</strong> Guests appear in your images and on guest lists without ever visiting your website. Say that you receive their details from the booking client and how a guest can contact you.</p>

    <p><strong>Print labs and subcontractors.</strong> Print labs, album makers, second shooters, and outsourced editors all receive client files. Name them as recipients.</p>

    <p><strong>Raw-file retention.</strong> Raw files hold every frame and full metadata. Give them their own retention period, not a vague "as long as necessary."</p>

    <h2 id="privacy-laws">Which Privacy Laws Apply to Photographers</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>GDPR and UK GDPR.</strong> These apply if your business is based in the EU or UK, or if you offer your services to people there.</li>
      <li><strong>CalOPPA (California).</strong> Any commercial website that collects personally identifiable information from California residents must post a privacy policy. In practice, this covers most US photographers with a contact form.</li>
      <li><strong>CCPA/CPRA (California).</strong> This applies only to businesses that meet its thresholds, such as annual revenue above roughly $26.6 million or handling the data of 100,000+ consumers. Most solo photographers fall below them.</li>
      <li><strong>COPPA (US).</strong> This applies if your website collects personal information directly from children under 13.</li>
      <li><strong>PIPEDA (Canada).</strong> This requires meaningful consent and limits collection to what you need.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Unsure whether <a href="/resources/do-i-need-a-privacy-policy-for-my-website/">your website needs a privacy policy at all</a>? A site with a contact form, analytics, or online payments almost always does.</p>

    <h2 id="where-to-display">Where to Display Your Privacy Policy</h2>

    <p>Link your policy in your website footer, next to the submit button on inquiry and booking forms, on gallery access pages, in email footers, and in your cookie banner if you use non-essential cookies.</p>

    <p>When your tools or workflow change, a <a href="/">free privacy policy generator</a> makes it quick to produce an updated version.</p>

    <RelatedGuides currentSlug="resources/privacy-policy-for-photography-website" />

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Is it a legal requirement to have a privacy policy on a website?</h3>
        <p>Often, yes. CalOPPA requires commercial websites that collect personal information from California residents to post one, and the GDPR and UK GDPR require privacy information whenever you collect personal data. Google Analytics' terms also require disclosure. A photography site with a contact form, gallery logins, or analytics should have one.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>What privacy policy is required for a website?</h3>
        <p>It should explain what personal data you collect, why, who receives it, how long you keep it, what rights people have, and how to contact you. GDPR-covered businesses must also state their legal bases. Photographers should cover bookings, model releases, image metadata, galleries, print labs, and payments.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can you provide an example of a privacy policy for a website?</h3>
        <p>Yes. The sample photography privacy policy on this page is a complete ten-section example covering data collected, uses, recipients, retention, children, rights, security, changes, and contact details. Copy it and replace the bracketed placeholders.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How do you write a simple privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>List the personal data you collect, explain why you use it, name the services that receive it, set a retention period for each type, describe people's rights, and give a contact email. Use plain language and describe what you actually do.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can I make my own privacy policy?</h3>
        <p>Yes. No law requires a lawyer to write it; it just has to describe your data practices accurately. Adapt a template like the one above or use a generator, and consider a lawyer's review if you handle unusual or sensitive data.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Can a photographer use my photos without consent?</h3>
        <p>It depends on the use and location. In the US, photographers usually own the copyright and can often use images in a portfolio, but using your likeness in advertising generally requires a model release. In the EU and UK, photos of identifiable people are personal data, so publishing them needs a lawful basis such as consent. Your contract usually sets the agreed uses.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Is it legal to use photos on a website without permission?</h3>
        <p>Generally, no. Photos are copyrighted from the moment they are taken, so you need a license unless fair use applies or the image is in the public domain. Commercial use of photos showing identifiable people may also need their consent or a model release.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</article>
</main>


